      "text": "To find the parameterization of the line, we can start by finding the slope of the line.\nThe slope of the line is $\\frac{3x - 5}{4}$ and since the line passes through the point $(3, 1)$, we can find the point on the line corresponding to $x = 3$.\nSubstituting $x = 3$ into the equation of the line, we have\n\\[y = \\frac{3(3) - 5}{4} = \\frac{7}{4}.\\]So, the point $(3, \\frac{7}{4})$ is on the line.\nWe want the distance between $\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ to be $t.$\nUsing the distance formula, we have\n\\[\\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{7}{4} - \\frac{7}{4} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{3x - 5}{4} - 1 \\right)^2} = t.\\]Simplifying, we have\n\\[t = \\frac{3x - 9}{4}.\\]Now we can find $\\mathbf{d}.$\nRecall that $y = \\frac{3x - 5}{4}$ and $y = \\frac{3x - 9}{4}.$\nSo, we have\n\\[\\frac{7}{4} = \\frac{3x - 9}{4}.\\]Simplifying, we find $x = 6.$\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{d} = \\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} \\end{pmatrix}}.$\nThe answer is: \\begin{pmatrix}6\\frac{7}{4}\\end{pmatrix}",
