      "text": "We can parameterize each of the two special points to obtain a two equation system for $\\mathbf{d}.$\n\nA parameterization of the intersection of the line with the $x$ axis is $\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix},$ so\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 + 4t \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.  \\tag{1}\\]A parameterization of the intersection of the line with the $y$ axis is $\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix},$ so\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 + 4t \\end{pmatrix}.  \\tag{2}\\]Solving (1) and (2) gives $t=3/4,$ $x=9/2,$ and $y=5.$\n\nThe parameter $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix}$ is at the intersection of the line with the $x$ axis, and $t \\mathbf{d}$ is a vector pointing from that point in the direction of the intersection of the line with the $y$ axis.  Therefore, $\\mathbf{d} = t( \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix} ) = \\begin{pmatrix} 3t \\\\ -t\\end{pmatrix}.$\n\nSince the distance between $\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ is $t,$\n\\[d = \\sqrt{\\left( x - 3 \\right)^2 + (y - 1)^2} = \\sqrt{t^2 + t^2} = \\sqrt{2t^2} = |t|\\sqrt{2} = |t| \\cdot 2,\\]so we have $t = \\pm 1/2.$  This gives $\\mathbf{d} = \\pm \\begin{pmatrix} 3/2 \\\\ -1/2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\boxed{\\pm \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}}.$",
