      "text": "Given the equation of the line \\( y = \\frac{3x - 5}{4}, \\) we need to find the parameterized form \\( \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\mathbf{v} + t \\mathbf{d}, \\) where \\( t \\) represents the distance from a point on the line to \\( \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}. \\)\n\nFirst, we identify a particular point on the line \\( \\mathbf{v} \\). To express it parametrically, we start by expressing the general form \\( x = k \\) and substituting \\( y \\) in terms of \\( k \\):\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{3k - 5}{4}.\n\\]\n\nWe need to avoid \\( x = 3 \\), so \\( k \\) must be chosen such that \\( x = 3 \\) is a value within the range of \\( y = \\frac{3x - 5}{4} \\). This requires \\( 3 \\ge 3 \\), simplifying to \\( k \\ge 5 \\). \n\nNow consider a point chosen at \\( k=5 \\):\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{3 \\cdot 5 - 5}{4} = \\frac{6}{4} = 1.5.\n\\]\n\nThus, point \\( (x, y) = (5, 1.5) \\) lies on the line. Setting this point to the form \\( \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 1.5 \\end{pmatrix} \\), the parametric form is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 1.5 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} d_1 \\\\ d_2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nEquating \\( y = \\frac{3x - 5}{4} \\) in our form:\n\n\\[\n1.5 = \\frac{3 \\cdot 5 - 5}{4} = \\frac{6 - 5}{4} = \\frac{1}{4}.\n\\]\n\nThis implies \\( y \\) increases linearly with \\( x \\) as expected. Hence the direction vector will have a negative differential in \\( y \\) (slope from \\( x = 5 \\) to \\( x = 3 \\)):\n\n\\[\nd_2 = \\frac{1.5 - 1}{5 - 3} = \\frac{0.5}{2} = \\boxed{0.25}.\n\\]\n\ncalculating \\( d_1 \\):\n\n\\[\nd_1 = -d_2 = -0.25.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) must be:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{bmatrix} 0.25 \\\\ -0.25 \\end{bmatrix},\n\\]",
